        "title": "Big Science - When Science Became an Industrial Enterprise",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "\"Big Science\" is Derek de Solla Price's term (1963) for the transition of science from individual or small-group \"Little Science\" to large institutionalized programs requiring massive funding, bureaucratic management, and political justification. The Manhattan Project was the template; everything since has been shaped by it."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-BIG-SCIENCE.md#003",
      "path": [
        "08-BIG-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:history-of-science:08-big-science, git-history:history-of-science:08-big-science]",
        "canonical_path": "history-of-science/08-BIG-SCIENCE.md",
        "concepts": "[big, science]",
        "current_path": "history-of-science/08-BIG-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:history-of-science:big-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "history-of-science",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[big, science]",
        "section": "history-of-science",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Big Science - When Science Became an Industrial Enterprise",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|              BIG SCIENCE -- THE SCALE TRANSFORMATION             |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                   |\n|  LITTLE SCIENCE                      BIG SCIENCE                 |\n|  (<1940)                             (1940 onward)               |\n|                                                                   |\n|  Individual investigator             Team of teams               |\n|  Single lab                          National laboratory         |\n|  Departmental funding                Government contracts        |\n|  Publication = communication         Classification + publication|\n|  Weeks to months                     Years to decades            |\n|  One country                         International collaboration |\n|  Results owned by researcher         Results owned by funder     |\n|  Mertonian CUDOS                     Secrecy + CUDOS tension     |\n|                                                                   |\n|  EXAMPLES:                                                        |\n|  Rutherford's lab (~20 people)        Manhattan Project (130,000) |\n|  Cavendish Lab (1 building)           CERN (~22,000 staff + users)|\n|  Marie Curie (she and Pierre)         Human Genome Project (intl) |\n|  Einstein at patent office            LHC (10,000 physicists)     |\n|                                                                   |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
